Eugene,

Do not contact Benson. Not yet.

Your exposure on the access logs is manageable if we move carefully.
The credential approval can be framed as routine CSO oversight -- you
authorised access, not execution. That distinction matters.

On the Da Vinci question: the "internal audit tool" framing is risky.
If forensics shows the salami logic in the code, that argument collapses
and we look worse for having made it. I'd advise against.

My recommendation: say nothing to anyone at Ellingson until I've had a
chance to review the Gibson logs myself. If Secret Service makes direct
contact, you call me immediately and say nothing beyond confirming your
identity.

We can get ahead of this. But only if you stop talking to people who
aren't me.

